🧩 Amazon EC2 Storage – Elastic Block Store (EBS), Snapshots, AMIs
🧠 1. Introduction to EBS
🔹 Definition

Amazon Elastic Block Store (EBS) provides persistent block-level storage volumes for EC2 instances.
You can think of it as a virtual hard drive attached to your EC2 server in the cloud.

Each EBS volume:

Lives within an Availability Zone (AZ).

Can be attached/detached to/from EC2 instances within that AZ.

Provides durability, scalability, backup, and encryption.

🔹 Key Features
Feature	Description
Persistent	Data remains after EC2 stop/start
Scalable	You can increase volume size or IOPS anytime
Snapshots	Point-in-time backup stored in S3
Encryption	Data encrypted in rest and transit
High Availability	Replicated within AZ
Multi-Attach	io1/io2 can be attached to multiple EC2s simultaneously
🔹 Use Cases

Databases (MySQL, PostgreSQL, MongoDB)

File storage for apps

Boot volumes for EC2

Data analytics workloads

⚙️ 2. EBS vs Instance Store
Property	EBS Volume	Instance Store
Persistence	Survives stop/start	Lost on stop/terminate
Backup	Snapshots supported	No backup
Attach/Detach	Yes	No
Resizing	Possible	Not possible
Cost	Pay for used GB	Included in instance price
Speed	Slight network latency	Extremely fast (local disk)
Use Case	Databases, web servers	Caching, temporary data

💡 Example:

Use EBS for an app’s database and logs.

Use Instance Store for temporary cache or buffers.

⚙️ 3. Creating and Attaching an EBS Volume
Step-by-Step (AWS Console)

Go to EC2 → Elastic Block Store → Volumes → Create Volume

Select:

Volume type: gp3

Size: 8 GiB

Availability Zone: same as your EC2 instance

Click Create Volume

After creation → select → Actions → Attach Volume

Choose EC2 instance → click Attach

Now your EC2 sees it as /dev/xvdf or similar.

Step-by-Step (AWS CLI)
# Create EBS volume
aws ec2 create-volume --availability-zone ap-south-1a --size 8 --volume-type gp3

# Attach volume
aws ec2 attach-volume --volume-id vol-0abcd1234ef567890 --instance-id i-0a12b34c56d789e01 --device /dev/xvdf

🧱 4. Mounting EBS Volume in Linux

SSH into your EC2 instance

ssh -i key.pem ec2-user@<public-ip>


List disks

lsblk


Format the disk

sudo mkfs -t ext4 /dev/xvdf


Create mount directory

sudo mkdir /data


Mount the disk

sudo mount /dev/xvdf /data


Check mount

df -h


Persist mount across reboots

Get UUID:

sudo blkid


Add to /etc/fstab:

UUID=<uuid> /data ext4 defaults,nofail 0 2

🔁 5. Detach and Re-Attach Volume
Detach

Unmount in Linux:

sudo umount /data


Console → Volumes → Actions → Detach Volume

Attach to another instance

Console → Attach Volume → Select New Instance

In new EC2 → run lsblk, mount again.

💡 EBS is AZ-specific:
You can attach only to EC2s in the same Availability Zone.

📏 6. Resize (Expand) EBS Volume

Console → Volumes → Modify Volume

Increase size or IOPS → Save.

In EC2:

lsblk
sudo growpart /dev/xvdf 1
sudo resize2fs /dev/xvdf1


✔️ File system now uses the new space.

🧩 7. Resize Root Volume

Root (boot) volume can also be resized:

Stop EC2 (optional).

Modify root volume size.

Restart EC2.

Inside instance:

sudo growpart /dev/xvda 1
sudo resize2fs /dev/xvda1

🧑‍🤝‍🧑 8. Multi-Attach EBS Volume

Only for io1/io2 volumes.

Up to 16 EC2s in same AZ.

Use cases:

Clustered file systems (Oracle RAC, Lustre).

High availability applications.

⚙️ 9. Types of EBS Volumes
Type	Description	Performance	Use Case
gp3	General Purpose SSD (default)	3,000 IOPS base	Most workloads
gp2	Older general SSD	3 IOPS/GB	Legacy
io1/io2	Provisioned IOPS SSD	Up to 64,000 IOPS	Databases
st1	Throughput optimized HDD	500 MB/s	Big data, logs
sc1	Cold HDD	250 MB/s	Backup, archive

💡 Always choose gp3 for general workloads, io2 for databases.

💾 10. Snapshots Overview

Snapshot = point-in-time backup of EBS volume.

Stored in Amazon S3 internally.

Snapshots are incremental — only changed blocks are saved.

🧩 Use Cases

Backup / Restore.

Copy to another region.

Create new EBS volumes or custom AMIs.

🧰 11. Create and Restore Snapshots
Create Snapshot

Console → Volumes → Actions → Create Snapshot → Enter name → Create.
OR

aws ec2 create-snapshot --volume-id vol-1234567890 --description "Daily backup"

Restore Snapshot

Console → Snapshots → Create Volume from Snapshot → Attach to EC2.

⚙️ 12. Automate EBS Backup (Lifecycle Manager)

EBS Lifecycle Manager (DLM):

Automates snapshot creation/deletion.

Define:

Target volumes (tags)

Schedule (daily/weekly)

Retention count

💡 Great for automatic compliance backups.

🧺 13. Snapshot & AMI Recycle Bin

AWS Recycle Bin lets you recover deleted:

Snapshots

AMIs
by setting retention rules (e.g., 7 days).

Steps:

Go to Recycle Bin in AWS Console.

Create retention rule.

Select resource type: Snapshot / AMI.

🌏 14. Copy Snapshot (Cross-Region / Cross-Account)

Console → Snapshots → Copy → Choose destination region.

Cross-account:

Modify snapshot permissions → Share with AWS Account ID.

Use for:

Disaster Recovery.

Migration between regions.

🔐 15. Encrypt EBS Volumes

Encryption using AWS KMS (Key Management Service).

Encrypts data at rest, in transit, and in backups.

When you copy an unencrypted snapshot → you can enable encryption.

Encrypted snapshots automatically create encrypted volumes.

💡 You can enforce encryption account-wide using service control policy.

🗑 16. Delete EBS Snapshots

Console → Snapshots → Select → Delete Snapshot.

Ensure no AMIs depend on it.

aws ec2 delete-snapshot --snapshot-id snap-0123456789

🖼 17. Amazon Machine Image (AMI)
🔹 What is AMI?

AMI = a pre-configured image that contains:

OS

Application software

Configuration
Used as a template for launching new EC2 instances.

🔹 Types

AWS-provided AMIs (Amazon Linux, Ubuntu, Windows)

Marketplace AMIs

Custom AMIs

🧰 Create Your Own AMI

Console → EC2 → Instances → Actions → Create Image.

Provide name and description.

Choose “No reboot” if required.

AWS creates a snapshot of your root and attached volumes.

💡 This snapshot becomes your AMI’s storage base.

🧩 Launch New EC2 from AMI

Go to AMIs → Select your image → Launch Instance.

Configure instance details → Launch.

🧾 Share or Delete AMI

Share: AMI → Modify permissions → Share with AWS account ID.

Delete: Deregister AMI + delete its snapshots.

🧠 18. Interview Questions – EBS, Snapshots, AMI
❓ Question	💡 Answer
What is EBS?	Block-level persistent storage for EC2 instances.
Difference between EBS and Instance Store?	EBS is persistent; Instance Store is ephemeral.
How do you increase EBS size?	Modify volume in console and resize file system inside instance.
What is a Snapshot?	Point-in-time incremental backup of an EBS volume.
Are snapshots stored in S3?	Yes, internally managed by AWS (not visible in S3 bucket).
How do you automate EBS backups?	Use EBS Lifecycle Manager (DLM).
Can EBS be attached to multiple instances?	Only io1/io2 Multi-Attach volumes.
What are EBS volume types?	gp3, gp2, io1/io2, st1, sc1.
What is an AMI?	Template image used to launch EC2 instances.
How is AMI created?	Snapshot of root volume + metadata.
How to share an AMI across accounts?	Modify AMI permissions or copy snapshot to target account.
Difference between Snapshot & AMI?	Snapshot = volume backup; AMI = full machine image.
Can we encrypt an existing unencrypted volume?	Yes, by copying snapshot and enabling encryption.
What is Multi-AZ for EBS?	EBS is AZ-specific; cross-AZ replication is done via snapshots or EFS.
What happens if EBS volume is deleted but snapshot exists?	You can recreate the volume from the snapshot anytime.
